WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:02.050
COLT MCANLIS: Google
Cloud Next 2017

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:05.450
hosted a huge number of talks
detailing the future of Cloud.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:07.450
If you didn't get a chance
to catch the session,

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:09.160
Saving Money on
Compute Engine, then

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.900
stay tuned, because
here's the recap.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:17.860
The pricing philosophy
for Compute Engine

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:19.840
is guided by three concepts--

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.570
RETO MEIER: Simplicity,
flexibility, and fairness.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:24.520
We aim to make pricing
as simple as we can

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.140
by offering
preconfigured products,

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.780
like N1 standard, that
can be easily customized

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:30.190
for your workload.

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:33.754
Similarly, we know that at its
essence a VM is CPUs and RAM.

00:00:33.754 --> 00:00:35.920
So another way we keep
things simple and transparent

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:37.780
is by not bundling
other components that

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:41.207
create opaque skews that
hide the true costs.

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:42.790
COLT MCANLIS: In
terms of flexibility,

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:45.760
we focus on the concepts
of pay for what you use

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:47.800
and buy only what you need.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.450
All clouds are pay as you use.

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.810
But the way you count usage
matters a lot in practice.

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:55.900
In GCE, we've had per
minute billing since day one

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:57.940
because at Google we
understand that workloads

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.890
don't come in hour long chunks.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:02.120
We also offer custom
machine types,

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.629
including the ability to
efficiently schedule jobs

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:06.700
and VMs of any size.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.100
A little over a year after
we launched this feature,

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.410
over 20% of all Compute
Engine workloads

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:13.660
are using custom machine
types and customers

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.697
are saving an average of
19% from this feature alone.

00:01:17.697 --> 00:01:19.780
RETO MEIER: When you use
our VMs consistently it's

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:20.730
more efficient for us.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:22.450
And we give those
savings back to you

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:24.580
through sustained use discounts.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:26.500
You still maintain
all the flexibility

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.150
of an on-demand cloud.

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:29.890
Start and stop instances
in whatever way

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:31.420
makes sense to your workload.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.820
And change your usage
pattern whenever you need.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:36.370
We'll give you the best discount
that your usage qualifies

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:38.030
for automatically.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.610
For pre-defined
instances, we use

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:41.740
a concept called
inferred instances

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:44.350
to calculate the biggest
savings that we can.

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.150
When we introduced
custom machine types,

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.130
we generalized this
model so it works

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:50.200
even with the thousands
of possible instance

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.266
shapes that you
can have with CMT.

00:01:52.266 --> 00:01:53.890
COLT MCANLIS: If the
graph on the right

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:55.930
is a picture of how
you actually ran

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:58.810
a series of five different VMs
of the same size and shape,

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.190
it doesn't look like you ran
any of them for very long.

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:04.670
Now, see, what we do here is
combine similar VMs over time,

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:07.150
as shown in the middle
picture, where we reinterpret

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:11.254
those same five VMs as if they
were three longer running VMs.

00:02:11.254 --> 00:02:13.420
When you look at those as
a percentage of the month,

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.430
you'll get the max
discount on one,

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:17.140
a healthy discount
on the second,

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.875
and the first tier of
discount on the third.

00:02:19.875 --> 00:02:21.750
RETO MEIER: And with
committed use discounts,

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.860
you can get our best
discount levels in exchange

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:26.260
for telling us which
resources you're guaranteeing

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:28.300
to use for the long-term.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.570
On predefined types, you can
get a 37% discount on one year

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:35.350
commitments and a 55%
discount on three years.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:38.560
For custom machine types,
the discounts are 40 and 57%

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:39.985
respectively.

00:02:39.985 --> 00:02:41.860
COLT MCANLIS: With our
right sizing features,

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.540
you can focus on bringing
your workload to the cloud.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:47.140
And once it's running, we'll
watch your CPU and memory

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:49.090
utilization and give
you recommendations

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.019
to help reduce costs.

00:02:51.019 --> 00:02:52.810
COLT MCANLIS: And by
using preemptible VMs,

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:54.670
a class of super
low cost VMs that

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:58.360
are optimized for short-term
use, you can save up to 80%

00:02:58.360 --> 00:02:59.940
off the list prices.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.190
Preemptible VMs are ideal for
fault tolerant, large scale

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:05.770
workloads that can handle
single workers occasionally

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:07.847
failing or disappearing.

00:03:07.847 --> 00:03:09.930
COLT MCANLIS: And finally,
there's our autoscaler.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:11.410
Because we don't
need pre-warming,

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:12.910
you don't have to
pay for idle time

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:14.620
while VMs are just
sitting around.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:17.460
Similarly, because we can
boot VMs in about 30 seconds

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.330
and scale down in
60 seconds later,

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:22.217
you're not paying for
extra boot or idle time.

00:03:22.217 --> 00:03:23.800
RETO MEIER: To hear
some real examples

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.780
of how customers spend
money in the cloud

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:27.940
and how the Google pricing
philosophy and features

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.550
described here can help
you save more money,

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:33.170
check out the full session
on the Google Cloud YouTube

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:33.670
channel.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:35.253
COLT MCANLIS: And
for more information

00:03:35.253 --> 00:03:36.760
on saving money
with Compute Engine,

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:38.470
check out the full
talk on YouTube.

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:41.251
If you're looking for more
recaps on great Next content,

00:03:41.251 --> 00:03:43.250
make sure to check out
the rest of our playlist.

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:46.180
And don't forget the Next
world tour coming soon

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.570
to a city near you.

